Instead of writing separate top and bottom loops, the full row index can be converted into a distance from the nearest edge. For a total of `2 * n` rows, that distance gives the number of missing stars.

With `n = 3`, the effective row values are `0, 1, 2, 2, 1, 0`. Therefore the star count becomes `3, 2, 1, 1, 2, 3`.

```csharp
class Solution{
    public void Pattern19(int n){
        int totalRows = 2 * n;

        for (int row = 0; row < totalRows; row++) {
            int distanceFromEdge = Math.Min(row, totalRows - 1 - row);
            int stars = n - distanceFromEdge;
            int spaces = 2 * distanceFromEdge;

            for (int col = 0; col < stars; col++) {
                Console.Write("* ");
            }

            for (int col = 0; col < spaces; col++) {
                Console.Write("  ");
            }

            for (int col = 0; col < stars; col++) {
                Console.Write("* ");
            }

            Console.WriteLine();
        }
    }
}
```

This version uses one outer loop, so the symmetry comes from `Math.Min` rather than from reversing a second loop. If `n` is `1`, `totalRows` is `2`, and both rows are printed correctly.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because each row prints up to `O(n)` characters.

Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The pattern has two matching halves. In the top half, the number of stars on each side goes down while the middle gap grows. The bottom half does the reverse.

For `n = 3`, the row sizes are `3, 2, 1, 1, 2, 3`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void pattern19(int n) {
        for (int row = 0; row < n; row++) {
            int stars = n - row;
            int spaces = 2 * row;

            for (int col = 0; col < stars; col++) {
                cout << "* ";
            }
            for (int col = 0; col < spaces; col++) {
                cout << "  ";
            }
            for (int col = 0; col < stars; col++) {
                cout << "* ";
            }
            cout << '\n';
        }

        for (int row = n - 1; row >= 0; row--) {
            int stars = n - row;
            int spaces = 2 * row;

            for (int col = 0; col < stars; col++) {
                cout << "* ";
            }
            for (int col = 0; col < spaces; col++) {
                cout << "  ";
            }
            for (int col = 0; col < stars; col++) {
                cout << "* ";
            }
            cout << '\n';
        }
    }
};
```

The top and bottom middle rows are printed twice, which matches the usual symmetric pattern. For `n = 1`, this prints two rows containing stars.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because the output contains a quadratic number of characters.

Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the output stream.

The top and bottom parts use the same row rule, but the bottom part starts with the smallest star count and grows. A small `printLine` method keeps the main method focused on that changing count.

The input array is not involved here, so there is no data to modify. For `n = 2`, the output has four rows: two stars per side, then one, then one again, then two again.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern19(int n) {
        for (int stars = n; stars >= 1; stars--) {
            printLine(stars, 2 * (n - stars));
        }

        for (int stars = 1; stars <= n; stars++) {
            printLine(stars, 2 * (n - stars));
        }
    }

    private void printLine(int stars, int middleSpaces) {
        for (int i = 0; i < stars; i++) {
            System.out.print("* ");
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < middleSpaces; i++) {
            System.out.print("  ");
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < stars; i++) {
            System.out.print("* ");
        }

        System.out.println();
    }
}
```

The two loops deliberately include the center pair of rows. That is why the smallest row appears twice, which is part of the required symmetric design. An input of `n = 0` produces no output.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The pattern can be treated as two phases. A recursive call prints the top part first, then another recursive call prints the bottom part while returning. The row number tells us both the star count and the gap size.

For `n = 3`, the recursive order is `0, 1, 2, 2, 1, 0`. That gives the required mirror shape.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern19(int n) {
        printRows(0, n);
    }

    private void printRows(int row, int n) {
        if (row == n) {
            printRows(n - 1, n);
            return;
        }

        printRow(n - row, 2 * row);

        if (row + 1 < n) {
            printRows(row + 1, n);
        }

        printRow(n - row, 2 * row);
    }

    private void printRow(int stars, int spaces) {
        for (int i = 0; i < stars; i++) {
            System.out.print("* ");
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < spaces; i++) {
            System.out.print("  ");
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < stars; i++) {
            System.out.print("* ");
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}
```

The stopping condition matters here. It prevents an extra recursive level when the lower half starts. The input is not changed, and `n = 1` still produces the two expected rows.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` due to printing all rows and columns.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursion stack.

